# Core Best Practices

- Follow existing architecture and naming conventions before introducing new patterns.
- Prefer small, focused changes with clear intent and minimal side effects.
- Preserve backward compatibility unless a breaking change is explicitly requested.
- Validate behavior with the narrowest useful test scope (unit first, then integration as needed).
- Never commit secrets, tokens, passwords, or environment-specific credentials.
- Keep comments concise and add inline comments only for complex business logic.
- Do not over-comment self-documenting code.
- If requirements are ambiguous, ask one precise clarifying question before implementing risky behavior.
- When changing APIs or data contracts, update related validation, types, and error handling together.
